#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079227} :activity/id #uuid "c69d16f5-44d0-5069-b44e-d2caf86ea506" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049806 :topic/id #uuid "156024ea-0497-5260-b6dc-995afd569210" :topic/math-academy-id 638} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079227 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079683 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $f(x)$ below:\n![](images/e4/e46fcaf79d93513c8b946de51ef451ad8a638936681ce3fc70ffdbe8de023078.svg)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ seems to have a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ However, by selecting a particular value for $k$, we can remove the jump discontinuity and make the function continuous.\n\nWe can find this value of $k$ by forcing the left and right-sided limits of the function to be equal at $x=-3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to (-3)^-} f(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x\\to (-3)^+} f(x)\n$$\n\nFor the left limit, we have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}(3{x}^{2}+kx) \\\\  & =3(-3)^{2}+k(-3) \\\\  & =27-3k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =(-3)^{3} \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the left and right limits as equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27-3k & =-27 \\\\ 3k & =54 \\\\ k & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to make $f(x)$ continuous at $x=-3,$ we need to choose $k=18.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "10dabe2c-998a-5731-bcf2-c0ed93bc7f2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 543 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "778b28a2-a45c-54f3-993a-34eba4c8d1b4" :step/math-academy-id 1021 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079229}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079228 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078382 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078220 :question/id #uuid "d17b0e14-aabc-5814-a242-d5fb803cb406" :question/math-academy-id "e-263"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55607d46-d93c